#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "87580982-1d5a-5f68-b9c8-df77b4da0196" :question/math-academy-id "e-5439" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})$." :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ we then need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nT\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFirst, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{3}{2})R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=-2y$.\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124244 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "f3694e9e-804f-55f0-9fb1-a78caec29224" :question/math-academy-id "e-5440" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))\n$$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))$, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124245 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "fc0725a9-354c-5e01-a924-12c0d2723ba3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5441" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})$." :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(u) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot k + 12 \\cdot 2 - 3 \\cdot 6 \\\\ -2 \\cdot k + 4 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\ 6 \\cdot k - 3 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3k + 6 \\\\ -2k - 4 \\\\ 6k + 12\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{3k + 6 = 0; - 2k - 4 = 0; 6k + 12 = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124273 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "5ba5ded2-bfca-5e53-9056-8aede726505f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5616" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$?" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(v_{1}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{2}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{3}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e495a5f6-0248-5a58-b5a8-ffa5ab5c9793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123143} {:answer/id #uuid "009e5efc-95fb-5966-95f6-d44fe76e54a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123144} {:answer/id #uuid "5522728d-9cb4-5dcc-8ec5-dd62426cd2dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123145} {:answer/id #uuid "76532239-cf78-5418-947a-b1b521b5e8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123146} {:answer/id #uuid "781ddf16-86f5-53be-a452-ad286ac28341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009e5efc-95fb-5966-95f6-d44fe76e54a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123144} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e10e1a3-7666-53e1-9810-7b16f963ddc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123142}] :question/id #uuid "c08047d4-78e2-5052-a86f-f3f6f58c0260" :question/math-academy-id "q-37825" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x - 2y \\\\ -10x + 5y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the value of $k$ such that $u ∈ Ker(T)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c37d69b7-b064-5cea-b2b2-463ae85c5522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123161} {:answer/id #uuid "ea29b905-c61f-53b2-a991-de3ce60ff466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123162} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe11a09-87e1-5271-a62e-6e583d413453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123163} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5eb3ac-40e4-5b06-af7d-9b417ba02ab1" :answer/t